# `once` means one time (`the job runs once`), but it also means after
# or when (`the write starts once the lock is held`). In the middle of a
# sentence, `runs once` reads as a complete count until the clause after
# it arrives, so the reader has to reread. The fix writes `after` or
# `when`.
#
# The rule counts `once` before a determiner (a word that introduces a
# noun, such as `the`, `a`, or `this`), a pronoun, or a proper noun,
# where it opens a clause. `a`, `an`, `every`, `each`, `per`, `more`,
# and `again` after it stay out, because each makes `once` a count
# (`once a day`, `once more`), and so do `or`, `and`, `in`, and `for`
# (`once or twice`, `once in a while`). A `once` that opens a sentence
# or follows a comma stays out too, because there it can only open a
# clause. Weir has no lookbehind, so the filter consumes the token
# before `once` and narrows the finding back to the word.
#
# It hit 83 lines in the measure.py corpus, and 20 of 20 sampled hits
# meant after or when.
expr main <(!(,)( )once( )<[DET, PRON, PROPN], ![a, an, every, each, per, more, again, or, and, in, for]>), once>
let message "Once. Write after or when."
let description "Flags once where it opens a clause in the middle of a sentence, where it also reads as one time."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
let scope "Sentence"
allows "Once the lock is held, the write starts."
allows "The job runs once a day."
allows "The hook runs once for each file."
allows "The write starts, once the lock is held."
